# Next substantive Vault contract: recoverable entry deletion

Recommend a bounded entry-only recycle-bin workflow next: ordinary deletion, a separate bin view, restoration to the previous group, and confirmed permanent deletion with native KDBX tombstones. Users currently have automatic history for edits but no compatible recovery path after deleting an entry. This next work changes useful product behavior rather than adding another recorder. It is a proposal only; no candidate implementation, original execution, runtime test or production change has occurred here.

The pinned source is KeePass 2.61.1 from source ZIP SHA-256 `711bd9ec7076661678469e51bae876debac07a08c32f1b1c6b3b8fe5e761c13a` (5,333,853 bytes; 1,381 entries). Ten selected source/license files were read with a 512 KiB per-file bound. `source-study.json` and `next-contracts.json` bind exact files, line/UTF-8 spans and context hashes. Selected source remains GPL-2.0-or-later with the exact released license; the complete ZIP is referenced locally, not republished in this documentation stage.

The release-bound manual discovery explicitly reports no recycle-text match in its 45 shipped pages. That is a finite search limitation, not proof that KeePass has no documented recycle behavior. No recycle requirement is invented under an existing `KPM2611` ID. The first-step manual group-deletion statement also does not specify destination/confirmation. The proposed `KPS2611-RECYCLE-*` IDs are new source-backed study identifiers, not additions to the frozen 199 ledger. Selected-history deletion is a smaller alternative with a direct manual record, `KPM2611-HISTORY-DELETE-SPECIFIC`; the history dialog keeps pending deletion in its editable history list until entry Save. It would need explicit durable/Cancel semantics rather than pretending the handler is immediate persisted deletion.

## Finite released contract to implement

- Resolve the bin by its UUID and group ancestry, never by display name. When enabled and missing, normal deletion creates a root-child bin with the TrashBin icon, Auto-Type and searching disabled. A bin UUID resolving to the root is rejected/replaced by the released helper. Preserve existing imported bin metadata and unrelated groups.
- Classify each entry before removal. Recycle is enabled unless the database disables it, explicit permanent deletion is requested (the original Shift path), or the entry is already in/under the bin. The original asks about permanent deletion and optionally confirms recycling according to UI configuration. A Studio-specific explicit confirmation may be a documented narrower interface; do not call it original dialog parity.
- Recycle removes the same entry from its parent and adds it to the bin with ownership/location tracking. Preserve UUID, password/protected flags, fields, attachments, history and unrelated unknown XML. Record the old parent's UUID as `PreviousParentGroup`; the original calls `Touch(false)`, updating access/usage and touching parents without changing the entry's content modification time. Do not create a content-history revision merely for moving the entry.
- Restore-to-previous-parent is available only when the previous UUID is nonzero, exists and differs from the current parent. The original operation removes the entry from its current parent, adds it to the previous group with location tracking, and records the former current parent's UUID as the new previous parent. It does not call `Touch` on this path. A deleted previous group must not silently redirect restoration to root.
- Permanent deletion removes the entry and adds a `PwDeletedObject` with the same UUID and deletion time. Do not mistake hiding the entry in the ordinary list for deletion, or omit tombstones from KDBX export.
- Separate the ordinary list from the bin view. Imported bin-descendant entries must have the same membership classification. Search inheritance and group/tag rules outside this explicit bin filtering remain open unless studied and implemented separately.

Whole-group recycle/delete, arbitrary group editing, emptying a bin containing groups, multi-database synchronization, original keyboard/dialog integration, localization and plugin events remain outside the first proposed slice. `EmptyRecycleBin` and recursive `PwGroup.DeleteAllObjects` are recorded as later contracts; an entry-only command must reject unsupported group contents before effects rather than discard them. Source-only analysis does not yet justify accepting malformed/cyclic group graphs or changing broader import behavior.

## Original comparison and acceptance design

Authenticate the exact release executable before each future original process/assembly load. Use fresh synthetic databases and owned temporary paths; do not invoke the original main-window delete handler against user data. Actual released `PwGroup.FindGroup`, entry-list removal, `AddEntry`, `Touch(false)`, `PwDeletedObject` and KDBX reader/writer methods can provide independent represented-data observations. The main-window orchestration and confirmation policy remain source-composed unless the original UI is actually executed. Count original assembly loads, actual method calls, composed orchestration steps, DOM-model groups, host restarts and browser checks separately. All such counts in this present source-only stage are zero.

Use deterministic identities and content, then compare metadata with explicit clock bounds: first deletion creates a bin; subsequent deletion reuses it; nested original groups restore by UUID despite duplicate names; existing bin descendants delete permanently; disabled recycling/permanent override adds tombstones; missing/same/zero previous parent refuses restoration; previous-parent tracking changes on restore; full history/attachment/custom/protected fields survive recycle/restore; content modification time stays unchanged while supported location/access/usage changes follow actual original helper behavior. Include multiple original-created and candidate-created KDBX round trips, and original re-open of candidate output. Do not normalize away mismatched group metadata or timestamp transitions.

The candidate should stage a private complete mutation, preflight duplicate/cyclic/over-depth structures, snapshot action input before queue awaits, and publish only after encrypted persistence succeeds. Confirmed permanence must be explicit. Existing selected entry, draft/session generations and cleanup boundaries must remain effective. Meaningful negative cases include stale/cross-key selection tokens, a disappearing previous group, failed/held persistence, duplicate UUIDs, over-budget tombstones/XML/history, repeated concurrent deletion/restore, Lock/disposal during a held response and restart after each durable state. Test ordinary visibility, actual restored bytes, no-op rejection and persistence failure atomically; no account/credential fixtures.

Use the existing local XML/node/depth/entry/record bounds initially and document them. A production implementation should not infer bin membership from a group path string or merely set a custom JSON `deleted` flag that the original cannot interpret. A staged adapter must update the native XML tree, metadata and flattened entry projection consistently. This representation review is the first feasibility gate before accepting a wider recycle workflow.

## Preserved evidence boundaries

This proposal does not alter the frozen history 1,454 stage, current-layout 41-group evidence, author replay 11, independent review 11/browser, earlier failed probes or old compatibility18 receipt. The copy-ready bundle's current 1,442-public/12-local mapping and 1,656 total public evidence count are separate from the frozen historical manifest. `INTEGRATION.md` in that bundle retains an earlier four-named-carrier sentence; its new receipt/current mapping, and this README proposal, state the total twelve accurately. Production integration and full-suite/browser evidence are root-owned subsequent work.
